    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §13-0327, En Con L",
    "lawSection": "Environmental Conservation Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S4855\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the environmental conservation law, in relation to\nextending the authority of the department of environmental\nconservation to manage scallops\n\nPurpose of the Bill:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to extend the authority of the Department\nof Environmental Conservation (DEC) to manage scallops.\n\nSummary of Provisions:\n\nSection 1 of this bill amends Environmental Conservation Law (ECL)\n13-0327 to extend, until December 31, 2018, DEC's authority to fix by\nregulation measures for the management of scallops (Family\nPectinidae).\n\nSection 2 of this bill provides that the bill shall take effect\nimmediately.\n\nExisting Law:\n\nECL § 13-0327 grants DEC regulatory authority for scallops until\nDecember 31, 2015.\n\nPrior Legislative History:\n\nDEC's regulatory authority to manage scallops was last extended by\nChapter 41 of the Laws of 2013.\n\nStatement in Support:\n\nNew York's bay scallop resource is primarily located in the waters of\nPeconic and Gardiners Bays on the eastern end of Long Island. This\nresource supports an important commercial fishery primarily located in\nPeconic Bays that provides economic benefits to the local economy and\nfishermen. The bay scallop resource also provides opportunities for\nrecreational harvest that are part of a maritime tradition on the east\nend of Long Island.\n\nDEC's regulatory authority for scallops is necessary in order to\npreserve the current regulatory program. Failure to extend the\nexisting regulatory authority by December 31, 2015 would result in the\nloss of regulatory protection for scallops.\n\nFailure to extend this authority would result in the uncontrolled\nharvest of bay scallops of any size which are presently at very low\npopulation levels and would not be protective of the resource and\nshellfish industry. This regulatory authority provides DEC with the\nflexibility to respond to biological and environmental events such as\nbrown tide that have a significant impact on the viability of the bay\nscallop resource. This authority can also be used to develop\nmanagement measures to support bay scallop restoration efforts that\n\n\nare taking place in the State waters of Peconic Bays and in town\nwaters.\n\nBudget implications:\n\nNone.\n\nLocal impact:\n\nThis bill would have no impact on local governments.\n\nEffective Date:\n\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n\n",
